.Dd $Mdocdate: August 2 2026 $
.Dt FuguLib::CLI 3p
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm FuguLib::CLI
.Nd subcommand dispatch for a command-line tool
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Bd -literal
use FuguLib::CLI;

my $cli = FuguLib::CLI->new(
    name    => 'mytool',
    usage   => '[-c file] <command>',
    options => { 'config|c=s' => 'the configuration file' },
    commands => {
        status => {
            summary => 'report the state',
            run     => sub ($cli, @argv) {
                say 'running';
                return FuguLib::CLI::EXIT_SUCCESS;
            },
        },
    },
);

exit $cli->run(@ARGV);
.Ed
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
dispatches the subcommands of a command-line tool.
A tool declares its global options once and its commands in a table.
The module parses the global options, finds the command, parses that
command's own options, and calls it.
Every command in a tool would otherwise repeat the same
.Xr Getopt::Long 3p
setup.
.Pp
Output has two channels, and they are not the same channel.
Data that a script reads goes to standard output; a command prints it
itself.
Diagnostics go to the logger, which writes to standard error.
Thus
.Ql mytool list --names | xargs
keeps working when the tool starts to warn about something.
.Ss new
.Fn new "%args"
creates a dispatcher.
.Pp
These are the arguments:
.Bl -tag -width "commandsXX"
.It Fa commands
The subcommand table, as a hash reference.
This argument is necessary.
.It Fa name
The program name in the usage line and in diagnostics.
The default is
.Ql cli .
.It Fa options
The global options, as a hash reference whose keys are
.Xr Getopt::Long 3p
specifications and whose values describe them.
.It Fa usage
The one line that follows
.Ql usage: <name> .
.It Fa log
The logger.
The default is
.Fn FuguLib::Log->default .
.El
.Pp
Each entry of
.Fa commands
maps a name to a hash reference:
.Bl -tag -width "summaryXX"
.It Va run
The body, as a code reference.
This key is necessary.
The body receives the dispatcher and the remaining arguments, and
returns an exit code.
.It Va usage
The argument summary of this command.
.It Va summary
One line for the command list.
.It Va options
This command's own options, in the same form as
.Fa options .
.El
.Ss run
.Fn run "@argv"
parses, dispatches, and returns the exit code of the command.
.Pp
An unknown command, a bad option, and a missing command all give
.Dv EXIT_INVALID_ARGS
with the usage line on standard error.
.Pp
A command name of
.Cm help ,
and an empty argument list, print the help and return
.Dv EXIT_SUCCESS .
Asking for help is not a failure.
Every command takes
.Fl -help ,
whether it declared the option or not.
.Ss option
.Fn option "$name"
returns a parsed option value.
The name is the first name of the specification, so
.Ql config|c=s
arrives as
.Ql config .
.Pp
Global options and the running command's options share one namespace.
A tool that gives the same name two meanings is a tool nobody can use.
.Ss options
.Fn options
returns every parsed option as a hash reference.
.Ss command
.Fn command
returns the name of the command that is running.
.Ss name
.Fn name
returns the program name.
.Ss log
.Fn log
returns the logger.
A command body reports through it.
.Ss print_help
.Fn print_help "$command"
prints the help for one command, or for the whole tool.
The help goes to standard output, because the user asked for it.
.Ss usage_error
.Fn usage_error
prints the usage line on standard error.
.Ss command_usage_error
.Fn command_usage_error "$command"
prints the usage line of one command on standard error.
.Sh EXIT CODES
The module defines the codes that every tool shares:
.Bl -tag -width "EXIT_INVALID_ARGSXX" -compact
.It Dv EXIT_SUCCESS
0
.It Dv EXIT_ERROR
1
.It Dv EXIT_INVALID_ARGS
2
.It Dv EXIT_CONFIG_ERROR
3
.It Dv EXIT_TIMEOUT
7
.El
.Pp
A code that belongs to one tool is defined by that tool, not here, and
starts above these.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn new
returns a dispatcher.
.Fn run
returns an exit code.
.Fn option
returns a value or
.Dv undef .
.Fn options
returns a hash reference.
.Fn name
and
.Fn command
return a string.
.Fn log
returns a logger.
.Sh EXAMPLES
This example declares a command with its own option:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
list => {
    summary => 'list the cached images',
    usage   => '[--names]',
    options => { 'names' => 'print names only' },
    run     => sub ($cli, @argv) {
        for my $entry (@entries) {
            say $cli->option('names')
                ? $entry->{name}
                : sprintf('%-20s %s', $entry->{name}, $entry->{size});
        }
        return FuguLib::CLI::EXIT_SUCCESS;
    },
},
.Ed
.Sh ERRORS
.Fn new
dies when the command table is absent, and when a command has no
.Va run
code reference.
Both are programming errors that a tool hits on its first run.
.Pp
.Fn run
does not die.
Every input failure gives
.Dv EXIT_INVALID_ARGS
and a usage line.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr FuguLib::Config 3p ,
.Xr FuguLib::Log 3p ,
.Xr Getopt::Long 3p
.Sh AUTHORS
.An Dick Olsson Aq Mt hi@senzilla.io
.Sh CAVEATS
The module does not catch a die from a command body.
A body that must not kill the tool catches its own errors.
.Pp
Global options must come before the command name, and a command's own
options after it.
The parser stops at the first argument that is not an option, which is
how a command can take an option name that the tool also uses.
